Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex","line":20,"producer_hash_method":"atlas-whitespace-normalized-text","producer_statement_sha256":"7b0ca4ddf68481d2a697695dee48ec17a753a19f1539452291f74e9d60ee8465","provenance":{"kind":"computed","pin":"fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb","source":"preprints/Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex"},"sha256":"sha256:d4d81c170ddf5f0859fa70a6485f547fdd03395d00cd12c81935e365a401fe2b","url":"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/fd4aeeb2ee4fc729c18d98444fed42fd0529eeeb/preprints/Every-complex-K3-surface-is-Oka-September-23-2026/build/sections/introduction.tex#L20"},"statement_kind":"theorem","text":"\\label{thm:main}\nLet $X$ be a complex K3 surface, with any smooth Hermitian distance\n$d_X$. For every integer $m\\geq1$, every nonempty compact convex set\n$K\\subset\\C^m$, every open neighborhood $U$ of $K$, every holomorphic\nmap $f:U\\to X$, and every $\\epsilon>0$, there exists a holomorphic map\n$F:\\C^m\\to X$ such that\n\\[\n                  \\sup_{z\\in K}d_X(F(z),f(z))<\\epsilon.\n\\]"}
